  "track.fp.preset.pure": "Functional programming · pure functions and the substitution model",
  "track.fp.m.pure.title": "fee() reading today's date, or fee(date) taking the date",
  "track.fp.m.pure.overview": "The clinic charges 200,000 VND on weekdays and 300,000 VND on Saturday and Sunday. We write the fee function two ways. Way 1: fee() looks up today's date by itself. Way 2: fee(date) is told the date by its caller. For each, we do two things: try replacing the call with its result, and run the same test on each day of the week of 23–29 November 2026.",
  "track.fp.m.pure.wrapup": "Way 1 is in trouble from the start: the same call fee() gives 200,000 one day and 300,000 another, so it cannot be replaced by a number. The test behaves the same: green Monday to Friday, red on Saturday and Sunday, though nobody changed the code. In way 2 every replacement works, and the test passes fixed dates, so it is green every day. The clock does not disappear; it just moves to where the function is called, in plain sight.",
  "track.fp.m.pure.simp.1": "The fee has only two levels, with no public holidays. The amounts are an example; the week of 23–29 November 2026 is the real calendar.",
  "track.fp.m.pure.simp.2": "The table records only the important replacements; a few obvious simplifications are merged to keep it short.",
  "track.fp.m.pure.ctl.hint": "Pick a version and step through. A red row in the table cannot be replaced; the bottom row shows whether the test is green or red on each day.",
  "track.fp.m.pure.ctl.case": "Version",
  "track.fp.m.pure.ctl.case.impure": "fee() looks up the date",
  "track.fp.m.pure.ctl.case.pure": "fee(date) is given the date",
  "track.fp.m.pure.diagramTitle": "What the function's result depends on",
  "track.fp.m.pure.diagramDesc": "The clock, the call, the value it returns and the test",
  "track.fp.m.pure.n.world": "Clock and counter",
  "track.fp.m.pure.n.call": "The fee call",
  "track.fp.m.pure.n.value": "Returned value",
  "track.fp.m.pure.n.test": "Tests",
  "track.fp.m.pure.met.subst": "Replaceable rows",
  "track.fp.m.pure.met.opaque": "Rows that cannot be replaced",
  "track.fp.m.pure.met.red": "Days with a red test",
  "track.fp.cap.pure": "Arrows show how information flows: the clock feeds the call, the call yields a value, the test checks that value. A red box is where the result depends on something outside the parameters.",
  "track.fp.m.pure.s.impure.s1.title": "Try replacing fee() with its result",
  "track.fp.m.pure.s.impure.s1.explain": "We have total = fee() + fee(). To work it out by hand, we replace fee() with the number it returns. But which number? On Monday it is 200,000, on Saturday 300,000. One call, two answers, so it cannot be replaced. This is the first sign of an impure function.",
  "track.fp.m.pure.ev.impure.s1": "Try replacing fee() with its result",
  "track.fp.m.pure.s.impure.s2.title": "Tidy up: val a = fee(); total = a + a",
  "track.fp.m.pure.s.impure.s2.explain": "This is a tidy-up everyone does: call once, use twice. For a normal function the result is identical. But fee() also secretly bumps the counter calls. Writing fee() + fee() adds 2; the tidy version adds only 1. The statistics are now wrong because of a clean-up.",
  "track.fp.m.pure.ev.impure.s2": "Tidy up: val a = fee(); total = a + a",
  "track.fp.m.pure.s.impure.s3.title": "Run the test on Monday: green",
  "track.fp.m.pure.s.impure.s3.explain": "The test checks fee() == 200,000. You run it on your machine on Monday 23 November: green. Reading the test, nobody can tell which day it checks, because the day lives in the machine's clock, not in the code.",
  "track.fp.m.pure.ev.impure.s3": "Run the test on Monday: green",
  "track.fp.m.pure.s.impure.s4.title": "The CI server reruns it on Saturday 28 November: red",
  "track.fp.m.pure.s.impure.s4.explain": "On Saturday night the CI server reruns the very same test, with no line changed. This time fee() returns 300,000 and the test is red. Run all week, it is red on exactly two days: Saturday and Sunday. A test that is green or red depending on the day cannot be trusted.",
  "track.fp.m.pure.ev.impure.s4": "The CI server reruns it on Saturday 28 November: red",
  "track.fp.m.pure.s.pure.s1.title": "Replace d with its value",
  "track.fp.m.pure.s.pure.s1.explain": "Now the function takes the date as a parameter. We set d to Saturday 28 November and substitute: total = fee(28 Nov) + fee(28 Nov). The result depends only on what we pass in, not on what day it is today.",
  "track.fp.m.pure.ev.pure.s1": "Replace d with its value",
  "track.fp.m.pure.s.pure.s2.title": "Work it out by hand down to one number",
  "track.fp.m.pure.s.pure.s2.explain": "Replace each call with the function body: 28 November is a Saturday, so the weekend condition holds, each call becomes 300,000 and the total is 600,000. The tidy version val a = fee(d); a + a also gives 600,000. The two forms are now truly the same.",
  "track.fp.m.pure.ev.pure.s2": "Work it out by hand down to one number",
  "track.fp.m.pure.s.pure.s3.title": "Tests with fixed dates: green",
  "track.fp.m.pure.s.pure.s3.explain": "Now the test picks its own dates: Monday 23 November must give 200,000, Saturday 28 November 300,000. Run on Monday: both green. A bonus: we can check the weekend price without waiting for the weekend.",
  "track.fp.m.pure.ev.pure.s3": "Tests with fixed dates: green",
  "track.fp.m.pure.s.pure.s4.title": "The clock moves outside; tests are green every day",
  "track.fp.m.pure.s.pure.s4.explain": "Reading the real clock and counting calls move to the layer that calls the function, the ViewModel. Run the test all week: no red day, because the test no longer touches the clock. Reading the clock still has to happen, but now in one obvious place (a later chapter of this track, not yet written, will go further).",
  "track.fp.m.pure.ev.pure.s4": "The clock moves outside; tests are green every day",
  "track.fp.m.pure.co.try": "Reading tip: at each step, check the table above to see if the replacement works, then the day row below to see if the test is green or red.",
  "track.fp.m.pure.co.impure.s2": "A function that returns the right number but also does something else (logs, bumps a counter) is not pure. Replacing the call with the number loses that extra work. [[src:book-fp-tut]]",
  "track.fp.m.pure.co.impure.s3": "A function that only reads data must always return the same result for the same input to be pure. So it may not look up the time, the date, random numbers or randomly generated IDs on its own. [[src:book-seemann]]",
  "track.fp.m.pure.co.impure.s4": "When a test flips between green and red, do not rerun it until it turns green. Find what the function reads from outside on its own, and turn it into a parameter.",
  "track.fp.m.pure.co.pure.s1": "In short: an expression is \"replaceable by its result\" if, anywhere it appears, swapping it for the result leaves the program running exactly as before. A pure function is one where every call is like that. [[src:book-fp-manning]]",
  "track.fp.m.pure.co.pure.s2": "This hand calculation is like solving an equation at school: replace each part with something equal to it until one number is left. It is called the substitution model. [[src:book-fp-manning]]",
  "track.fp.m.pure.co.pure.s3": "The biggest gain of a pure function is easier reading: once it returns, you only need to remember the result, not how it was computed. [[src:book-seemann]]",
  "track.fp.m.pure.co.pure.s4": "This arrangement is called \"a pure core with a thin shell\": the decisions receive everything through parameters; only the outermost layer looks at the clock, logs or calls the network. [[src:book-seemann]] [[src:book-fp-manning]]",
  "track.fp.vz.tab.pure": "Step-by-step replacement table",
  "track.fp.vz.pure.cap": "Each row is the program after replacing one part with its value. A red row: after the replacement the program is no longer the same. Outlined: added in this step.",
  "track.fp.vz.pure.tcap": "Running the same test on each day of the week of 23–29 November 2026. \"?\": not run on that day yet.",
  "track.fp.vz.pure.legend": "Red hatched: cannot be replaced, or a red test. Bold: replaceable, or a green test. \"?\" cells open step by step.",
